<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>Amnesty International UK is one of the largest human rights organisations in the world, with over half a million supporters across the UK. They came to us to research a new website and Knowledge Hub. But from the first conversation, it was clear we couldn't look at those platforms in isolation.</p>
  <p>Users don't care whether something lives on the website or a separate hub. They just want to get what they need without hitting dead ends. So we pitched a broader discovery: one that covered the website, the planned Knowledge Hub, an online community and the overall digital strategy connecting them. AIUK said yes.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="amnesty-what">
  <h2 id="amnesty-what">What I did</h2>
  <p>I led the user research end to end across 20 in-depth interviews, spanning activists, educators, long-standing volunteers, casual supporters and people with little or no prior connection to Amnesty. I ran desk research across internal strategy documents, audience segmentation data and existing consultation findings to map what AIUK already knew and where the gaps were. I manually coded and synthesised all interviews, then delivered findings and recommendations through a written report and stakeholder playback.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="amnesty-problem">
  <h2 id="amnesty-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>AIUK's digital estate had become disjointed. The website was out of date. The Knowledge Hub and Online Community didn't exist yet. Internally, teams were working in silos around unresolved questions: who are our audiences really? What should live where? How do we make it feel seamless?</p>
  <p>Meanwhile, users were finding their own workarounds. Activists felt disconnected. Teachers were downloading PDFs and giving up. Supporters didn't know where to go or what to do next.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="amnesty-found">
  <h2 id="amnesty-found">What we found</h2>
  <p>The research surfaced something that matters well beyond Amnesty. People who care deeply about human rights often don't see themselves as activists. They sign petitions, share content privately, donate when something moves them, attend local events. But the word "activism" felt too big, too expert, too confrontational.</p>
  <blockquote><p>I wouldn't consider myself particularly an activist.</p></blockquote>
  <p>That gap between behaviour and identity is a design problem. If your platform only speaks to people who already see themselves as part of the movement, you're leaving a huge audience behind.</p>
  <p>People were also arriving via Google and email, rarely from the homepage. Even regular supporters said they'd just type "Amnesty UK" into Google rather than navigate the site, because the on-site search was so unreliable.</p>
  <blockquote><p>If you went into the Amnesty website and used the search facility there, you would never, never find what you're looking for.</p></blockquote>
  <p>Engagement dropped off not because people stopped caring, but because the platform kept failing them at the moment they tried to act. No clear next step. No feedback on what their action achieved. No sense that anyone noticed they were there.</p>
  <blockquote><p>If there was some kind of way of tracking it... like a Spotify Unwrapped, but for activism... I would love that.</p></blockquote>
  <p>And for some, the barriers were even more fundamental. People with disabilities or health conditions described digital participation as their only real route into the movement, and the platform wasn't designed with them in mind.</p>
  <blockquote><p>Often we get left out because we can't make meetings... or because we have disabilities... and I don't think that's fair.</p></blockquote>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="amnesty-changed">
  <h2 id="amnesty-changed">What changed</h2>
  <p>The research gave AIUK something they'd been missing: a shared, evidence-based understanding of who their audiences actually are and what gets in their way. Teams that had been working in silos came out of the process aligned. The findings fed directly into sprint planning, user stories and content structure for the new website and Knowledge Hub.</p>
  <p>The insight that people arrive via Google rather than the homepage shifted how the team thought about entry points entirely. Campaign pages and resource landing pages were redesigned to work as standalone destinations, not way stations back to the homepage.</p>
  <p>The finding that supporters want to know what their actions achieved led to new content patterns: feedback loops built into petition journeys, impact messaging surfaced after donations, clearer signposting of what happens next.</p>
  <p>The distinction between warm and cold audiences, which the research mapped clearly for the first time, shaped the information architecture. Different entry points, different content framing, different calls to action depending on where someone was in their relationship with Amnesty.</p>
  <p>And the language work mattered too. Terms like "Knowledge Hub" and "Online Community" tested poorly across almost every participant group. Plain language alternatives were adopted across the site.</p>
  <p>Torchbox was commissioned to lead the full redesign and replatform. The new site launched in 2025.</p>
